// SplitLiveRanges — the cold-spill live-range splitter (design: ARCHITECTURE.md's
// register-allocator section, Rule 2). It is what relieves COLD pressure, so the colorer
// never faces "pressure > pool": a value that is idle across a
// region of high register pressure is SPLIT — it lives in memory across the gap and in a
// register only where it is used — so the colorer then always finds `maxPressure ≤ pool`.
// HOT pressure (a value a loop USES that still cannot fit) is the E5001 diagnostic —
// `chooseVictim` returns `none` and the driver throws `raiseRegisterPressure` (M5.7,
// RegisterPressureDiagnostic.maxon).
//
// THE TWO INVARIANTS THIS RESTS ON (design Rules 1 + 2):
//
//  • BELADY SPLIT AT THE EVICTION POINT — a spilled value's live range is split ACROSS THE
//    PEAK, not shipped whole to memory. The store goes after the value's LAST use BEFORE the
//    peak (or at its def when it is idle before the peak); the reload goes before its NEXT
//    use AFTER the peak. Only the uses AT/AFTER the split point are rewritten to the fresh
//    reload id — the uses BEFORE it keep the ORIGINAL value in its original register. This is
//    what makes the value DEAD across the peak (freeing its register there), so a split
//    actually LOWERS peak pressure and the loop converges. Splitting whole-range and reloading
//    before a block's FIRST use (the earlier design) is WRONG: a value used both before AND
//    after a peak would get its fresh reload before the pre-peak use, leaving that fresh value
//    live ACROSS the peak — pressure never drops and `chooseVictim` re-picks the same lineage
//    forever.
//
//  • DOMINATING RELOADS — the key insight that retires v1's SplitKit failure with NO SSA
//    reconstruction. Every reload is placed in a use's own block, before the first AFTER-peak
//    use in that block — which DOMINATES the after-peak uses it serves. So no merge ever sees
//    two definitions, NO phi is ever needed, and each reload just DEFINES A FRESH ValueId whose
//    after-peak uses are rewritten to it (keeping the program in SSA and Rule 1 intact). The
//    price is occasional duplicate reloads (one per after-peak use-block), all provably COLD.
//
//  • RULE 2 (spill placement) — a store or reload may be inserted at a point `q` only if,
//    for every loop containing `q`, the value has NO use or def inside that loop. This is
//    what makes cold spills cold: spill code is NEVER added to a loop body for a value the
//    loop uses. We guarantee it by only ever choosing a victim whose def AND every use are
//    at loop depth 0 (`isColdSpillable`), and placing the store and each reload at (depth-0)
//    use/def sites — so no store/reload ever lands in any loop at all. `assertPlacementLegal`
//    re-verifies this on every inserted op: a violation would be a HOT spill that should have
//    been E5001, so it panics rather than ship. A value idle across a loop it does not touch
//    is exactly the depth-0-def, depth-0-uses shape, split with ZERO instructions in the loop
//    body — the design's signature case.
//
//    RULE 2 AS AMENDED — refuse the SEARCH, not the SPILL. The paragraph above is the rule for a
//    FULL-POOL overflow only. At a CONFINED one (Hall's condition against a proper subset of the
//    pool: values a call denies the 9 caller-saved registers, or an `idiv` denies RAX/RDX) the value
//    CANNOT stay in a register across the clobbering op, so memory is not a choice the allocator
//    searches for — it is the only placement that exists. Such a bracket is therefore emitted at ANY
//    loop depth (`SpillPlacement.forced`), and it is never E5001: the program fits the machine, and
//    the restructuring E5001 would demand emits the same load and store at every use anyway. See
//    `SpillPlacement`, `SplitScope`, and `noVictimAtPeak`.
//
// REMAT (load-bearing, not an optimization): an integer constant (`movRegImm`/`movRegImm32`)
// is REMATERIALIZED rather than spilled — re-emitted at each use with a fresh ValueId, never
// a register across the gap and never memory. A constant is always PREFERRED over spilling a
// real value (remat is free — no store, no slot, no reload). shv2 has no `xor reg,reg` zero
// idiom yet (StdToX64Conversion emits `movRegImm32 0`), so only the two `movRegImm` forms are
// rematerializable here; rdata/witness-table address remat is a later-milestone class.
//
// ALGORITHM (iterative Belady/MIN with the dominating-reload restriction): recompute
// liveness; while some program point OVERFLOWS ITS POOL, find that PEAK op, choose a
// relievable value crossing it — a constant to rematerialize if one is available, else
// the value with the FARTHEST next use (Belady/MIN) — and split its live range AT the peak;
// repeat. Splitting at the peak makes the victim DEAD across the peak op, so pressure there
// drops by one and the loop converges (a store/reload only READS an already-live value / only
// SHIFTS which value is live, so neither ever raises the function's max pressure).
//
// A victim whose live range the eviction-point split does NOT cut — a loop-invariant a call inside
// the loop reads on BOTH sides of itself, so it is live across the call around the back edge no matter
// how its after-peak uses are rewritten — is relieved at a CONFINED peak by rewriting EVERY use
// instead (`SplitScope`). That is the case `noVictimAtPeak`'s confined panic used to hit.
//
// "OVERFLOWS ITS POOL" is per-point AND per-VALUE, because a clobber SHRINKS a value's pool: a value
// live across a call is forbidden all 9 caller-saved registers, so its only home is one of the 5
// callee-saved; one live across an `idiv` is confined to `pool ∖ {RAX,RDX}`. Values at one point can
// therefore be shopping in differently sized register sets, and no single number decides whether they
// fit. `analyzePressure` tests HALL'S CONDITION on those per-value pools (`HallCondition`): no
// register subset may have more values CONFINED to it than it has registers. That subsumes the old
// "compare the live count against `pool ∖ op.implicitDefs` AT the clobber op" check and additionally
// sees what that check structurally could not — values confined by DIFFERENT calls, colliding at a
// point that is not itself a call. `maxPressure ≤ pool` is NECESSARY but far from SUFFICIENT.
//
// ==========================================================================
// PERFORMANCE: EVERY PER-VALUE QUESTION IS ANSWERED FROM AN INDEX, NEVER A SCAN
// ==========================================================================
//
// `chooseVictim` asks the same handful of questions — "where is it defined?", "where is it
// used?", "is it a phi?", "is it edge-passed?", "is it already stored?", "does it touch a
// loop?" — of EVERY value crossing the peak. Answering each with its own walk of the function
// made the splitter O(candidates × blocks × ops) PER SPLIT, and it was 61% of register
// allocation whenever it ran. The function's `UseIndex` (TargetLiveness) answers all of them in O(1) (or O(uses of the one
// value)) from dense columns + a CSR use list keyed by value id, built in ONE pass over the
// function — the same counting-sort idiom as `TargetLiveness.buildUseIndex`, and for the same
// reason. The general rule that section states holds here too: **a sparse set is never walked
// by iterating the whole value space** — `chooseVictim`'s candidates come from
// `bitsetCollectRow` over the peak's live set, not from `for v in 0 upto valueCount`.
//
// HOW THE INDEX STAYS CORRECT ACROSS THE SPLITTER'S IR MUTATIONS: it is not maintained — it is
// a per-iteration SNAPSHOT, rebuilt (with liveness) after every split. Within one iteration the
// ONE consumer that runs while the IR is being mutated is `spillValue` / `rematerializeConstant`,
// and it reads only the two fields that are STABLE under the mutations they make: an op's
// `module.ops` index (the table is append-only, so an existing index never moves) and a use's
// owning block (insertion never moves an op between blocks). Everything positional — a
// `block.opRefs` position, a global layout sequence number — is re-derived at the point of use,
// exactly as the pre-index code did with its own use-site snapshot. So a split cannot read a stale
// index, because nothing it reads can go stale.
//
// A NECESSARY-simplicity note: unlike the design's single-sweep Braun–Hack MIN, this still
// recomputes LIVENESS once per split (the CFG itself is invariant under splitting and IS hoisted
// out of the loop — see `splitLiveRanges`). Making liveness incremental would need the per-block
// live sets to be editable in place, which the CSR representation is deliberately not; that is
// the remaining super-linear term (O(function × splits)) and the natural next step.

// The outcome of a lineage lookup: `source(id)` for a splitter-minted fresh value (the
// value it derives from), `none` for any value the splitter did not mint. A union rather
// than a sentinel id — a real ValueId 0 would be indistinguishable from "not found".
export union SplitLineageLookup
	none
	source(sourceId ValueId)
end 'SplitLineageLookup'

// SplitLineage — maps every fresh ValueId the splitter mints back to the ORIGINAL user value
// it derives from: a reload's `loadRegSlot` def -> the value it reloads, a rematerialized
// constant's re-emitted def -> the constant it re-emits. This is what lets the E5001
// diagnostic resolve a fresh id in a blocking set to a SOURCE origin (Rule 3): a fresh id has
// no `ValueOrigin` (it is minted after parsing) and is not a phi, so without this chase it
// would hit the Rule-3 "no origin" panic even though the value the author wrote — the spilled
// variable, the constant — is right there in source. The chase is transitive (a reload of a
// reload, a value spilled then its reload rematerialized), which the diagnostic follows to a
// value that DOES have a source origin (the original constant / spilled user value / a phi).
//
// Two dense scalar columns (fresh id, source id), pushed in lockstep, scanned linearly. This
// one genuinely IS cold and stays a scan: the ONLY caller is the E5001 diagnostic, which runs
// once per compile and then THROWS — it is not on any path a successful compile takes.
export type SplitLineage
	var freshIds as ValueIdArray
	var sourceIds as ValueIdArray

	export static function create() returns SplitLineage
		return Self{freshIds: ValueIdArray.create(), sourceIds: ValueIdArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Record that `freshId` (a reload or rematerialized def) derives from `sourceId`. The two
	// columns are only ever pushed together here, so they stay the same length by construction.
	export function record(freshId ValueId, sourceId ValueId)
		self.freshIds.push(freshId)
		self.sourceIds.push(sourceId)
	end 'record'

	// The value `freshId` derives from, or `none` when `freshId` is not splitter-minted (a
	// parser value / phi). First match is authoritative — each fresh id is recorded once.
	export function sourceOf(freshId ValueId) returns SplitLineageLookup
		for i in 0 upto self.freshIds.count() 'each'
			let fresh = try self.freshIds.get(i) otherwise panic("SplitLineage.sourceOf: freshIds.get OOB at {i}")
			if fresh == freshId 'match'
				return SplitLineageLookup.source(try self.sourceIds.get(i) otherwise panic("SplitLineage.sourceOf: sourceIds.get OOB at {i}"))
			end 'match'
		end 'each'
		return SplitLineageLookup.none
	end 'sourceOf'
end 'SplitLineage'

// The peak-pressure program point: the op with the maximum live-before population, its owning
// local block, and the live-before set there (the candidate spill pool). `found` is false when
// no op's live-before count exceeds the pool (nothing left to split).
export type PressurePeak
	export var found as bool
	export var pressure as RegCount
	export var blockLocal as CfgIndex
	export var liveBefore as BitWordArray
	export var peakSeq as ByteCount
	// Which of the two overflows this point is — the distinction the amended Rule 2 turns on.
	//
	//  • CLOBBER-ONLY (`true`): the live values fit the FULL pool, but more of them are CONFINED to
	//    some proper subset of it than that subset has registers (Hall's condition, `HallCondition`).
	//    They fit the machine — they simply cannot all sit in the registers left to them by the ops
	//    they are live across (a call clobbers the caller-saved set; `idiv` takes RAX/RDX). Relief is
	//    a FORCED store/reload bracket: a confined value cannot stay in a clobbered register, so
	//    memory is the ONLY placement — there is nothing to search. Permitted at ANY loop depth, and
	//    never E5001.
	//
	//  • FULL-POOL (`false`): pressure exceeds the full pool outright. This is genuine register
	//    demand — the loop's working set is larger than the machine — and only COLD splitting
	//    (a value idle across the region) relieves it. When nothing is cold, THAT is E5001.
	//
	// `analyzePressure` always prefers the higher-pressure peak, and a full-pool overflow is by
	// definition higher than any clobber-only one, so full-pool peaks are relieved FIRST. That
	// ordering is what lets `storeFitsPool` be sound at a clobber peak (see `chooseVictim`).
	export var clobberOnly as bool
	// The register set the overflow is measured against — Hall's WITNESS. `pool` itself for a
	// full-pool peak (every value is confined to the pool, so the overflow is plain pigeonhole);
	// a PROPER SUBSET for a clobber-only one — the 5 callee-saved registers when the confining op is
	// a call, `pool ∖ {rax,rdx}` at an `idiv`.
	//
	// Two things read it, and both would be wrong without it. `chooseVictim` relieves the peak by
	// spilling a value CONFINED to the witness — spilling one that could have taken a register
	// outside it frees nothing the confined values were able to use. And the E5001 deficit is
	// `pressure - popcount(witness)`: measured against any other pool it is not the number of values
	// the author must actually remove.
	export var witness as RegMask
	// Is the peak op ITSELF a fixed-register point (does it carry implicit clobbers)? The ranking
	// tie-break — see `peakOutranks`.
	export var atFixedRegisterOp as bool

	static function create(found bool, pressure RegCount, blockLocal CfgIndex, liveBefore BitWordArray, peakSeq ByteCount, clobberOnly bool, witness RegMask, atFixedRegisterOp bool) returns PressurePeak
		return Self{found: found, pressure: pressure, blockLocal: blockLocal, liveBefore: liveBefore, peakSeq: peakSeq, clobberOnly: clobberOnly, witness: witness, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp}
	end 'create'
end 'PressurePeak'

// How a spill's store/reload placement is constrained — the amended Rule 2 in one type.
//
//  • `cold` — the classic shape: every inserted op must land at loop depth 0 (asserted by
//    `assertPlacementLegal`), so no spill code EVER enters a loop body. This is what relieves a
//    FULL-pool overflow, and it is free: the store runs once before the loop, the reload once
//    after it.
//
//  • `forced` — relief at a CONFINED point, permitted at any loop depth. The value cannot survive
//    the ops it is live across in a register those ops clobber, so MEMORY is the only home left to
//    it — the allocator makes no choice and searches nothing. Rule 2 admits it because the code is
//    CHEAP AGAINST THE OP IT BRACKETS: a load and a store against a call that costs far more.
//    Refusing it would send the author to hand-write the same load and store (a value hoisted into
//    an array is loaded and stored at each use) — strictly worse code, for an error the compiler
//    could have avoided emitting.
//
//    PLACEMENT is not SCOPE. This says the spill code may land inside a loop; `SplitScope` says which
//    uses it serves. A value read only AFTER the clobber gets the store/reload bracket around it; one
//    read on BOTH sides of the clobber, and so live across it around the back edge, gets a load at
//    each use (`SplitScope.everyUse`) — still forced, still unsearched, just paid per use.
enum SpillPlacement
	cold
	forced
end 'SpillPlacement'

// WHICH of a victim's uses a split moves to memory — the second half of a relief decision, and the
// half the peak's PLACEMENT does not decide.
//
//  • `afterPeakUses` — the Belady split at the eviction point, and the shape every relief has when it
//    is available: the after-peak uses are rewritten to reloads, the before-peak uses keep the
//    ORIGINAL value in its original register. It leaves the value dead across the peak — which is the
//    entire point — PROVIDED no before-peak use is still reachable FROM the peak (`killsValueAtPeak`).
//
//  • `everyUse` — EVERY use is rewritten, before-peak ones included, so the original's only remaining
//    reader is its own store and it is dead from the def onward. This is what a value whose before-peak
//    use is reachable from the peak AROUND A BACK EDGE needs, and it is the only thing that works
//    there: a loop-invariant read both before and after a call inside the loop is live across that
//    call on the back edge no matter how its after-peak uses are rewritten, so the partial split
//    relieves NOTHING (`killsValueAtPeak` correctly refuses it). Six such values against the five
//    callee-saved registers fit the machine easily — the ABI simply denies them the nine caller-saved
//    ones — so refusing to relieve them is neither E5001 (the program fits) nor a panic: it is the
//    forced bracket, paid at every use instead of once. The value is loaded before each use and lives
//    in a register only there, which is exactly the code an author would hand-write for it.
//
// The cost difference is why `chooseVictim` prefers an `afterPeakUses` victim whenever one exists.
enum SplitScope
	afterPeakUses
	everyUse
end 'SplitScope'

// Does a split of this scope leave the BEFORE-peak uses reading the original value in its register?
function keepsOriginalBeforePeak(scope SplitScope) returns bool
	return match scope 's'
		afterPeakUses gives true
		everyUse gives false
	end 's'
end 'keepsOriginalBeforePeak'

// The result of the per-iteration pressure sweep: the peak-pressure op (where a victim is
// chosen) and the per-value def-point live count (the pressure each value's spill store would
// face). Both come from ONE backward sweep — a value's live-AFTER-def is captured before the
// backward step, the peak's live-before after it — so the two are never recomputed separately.
type PressureAnalysis
	export var peak as PressurePeak
	export var defLiveAfter as CfgIndexArray

	static function create(peak PressurePeak, defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray) returns PressureAnalysis
		return Self{peak: peak, defLiveAfter: defLiveAfter}
	end 'create'
end 'PressureAnalysis'

// A chosen victim + how to relieve it.
union SpillChoice
	// No value crossing the peak can be relieved. At a FULL-POOL peak that is the HOT overflow the
	// design exists to report (E5001, `raiseRegisterPressure`); at a CONFINED one it is a splitter
	// bug — see `noVictimAtPeak`.
	none
	// Rematerialize a constant (re-emit it before each rewritten use, no slot, no store).
	remat(valueId ValueId, scope SplitScope)
	// Spill a value, splitting its live range so it is dead across the peak: a store at its def
	// (skipped when an earlier split already wrote it to its slot) and a reload before each
	// rewritten use.
	spill(valueId ValueId, scope SplitScope)
end 'SpillChoice'

// ==========================================================================
// The driver
// ==========================================================================

// Split one function's live ranges so the colorer finds every program point within its pool.
// Mutates the IR (inserts spill/reload/remat ops, rewrites reloaded uses to fresh values) and
// returns the final liveness (recomputed on the split IR, `maxPressure ≤ pool` guaranteed) so
// the caller reuses it for coloring. Called by `allocateFunction` BEFORE coloring.
//
// `throws CompileError` because a HOT overflow — a program point over its pool where NO
// crossing value is cold-spillable — is E5001 (`raiseRegisterPressure`), not a placeholder
// panic. `project` (its `opRanges`/`valueOrigins` tables) + `funcIndex` are what the E5001
// diagnostic maps each blocking Target ValueId back to its source span through.
export function splitLiveRanges(module TargetModule, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, topo FuncTopology, pool RegMask, stats RegAllocStats, project Project) returns LivenessResult throws CompileError
	let poolSize = regMaskPopcount(pool)
	// Fresh-id -> source-value map for the E5001 diagnostic: every reload/remat id the loop
	// below mints is recorded here so a fresh id in a blocking set resolves to source, not a
	// Rule-3 panic (see SplitLineage).
	var lineage = SplitLineage.create()
	var nextSlot = 0 as SpillSlot

	// THE CFG IS INVARIANT UNDER SPLITTING, so it is built ONCE and reused by every liveness
	// recomputation below. A split only ever inserts `storeSlotReg`/`loadRegSlot`/`movRegImm`
	// BODY ops, rewrites operands, and repoints branch-edge ARGS — it never adds or removes a
	// block, never changes a `jcc`/`jmp` target, and so cannot change the block set, the
	// successor/predecessor edges, or the loop depths. Rebuilding it per split re-scanned the
	// whole function, re-derived both CSR graphs, and re-ran the natural-loop walk once per loop
	// header — a per-split cost proportional to the function that bought nothing. `valueCount` is
	// the ONE thing that changes (fresh reload/remat ids), and the driver knows it exactly: every
	// minted id is materialized by an op it inserts, so `nextValueId` IS what a rescan would find.
	//
	// The SKELETON is not even built here: `reorderFuncBlocksRpo` just built it (and it is the
	// caller's `topo` argument), and it has been valid ever since — the reorder is the last pass
	// that touches the block list. So all that is added on top is the loop depths and the value
	// count, which the reorder had no use for and therefore never paid for.
	var cfg = buildFuncCfg(module, func: func, topo: topo)
	var nextValueId = cfg.valueCount
	var liveness = timedLiveness(module, cfg: cfg, func: func, stats: stats)

	// TERMINATION. Each iteration splits one value at the worst OVER-POOL op: the victim goes to
	// memory ACROSS that op (dead there), so the op's pressure drops by exactly one, while NO other
	// op's pressure rises (a store/reload only renames an already-live value over a region it was
	// live in anyway — it never lifts the function's max pressure). So the potential
	// Φ = Σ_ops max(0, effectivePressure(op) − reducedPool(op)) — a non-negative integer — STRICTLY
	// DECREASES every iteration and the loop MUST reach Φ = 0 (no over-pool op → `peak.found` false).
	// This holds under MULTIPLE splits per lineage too: a reload is itself an ordinary value the next
	// iteration may split again (via a fresh id), each such split cutting a strictly smaller run of
	// uses. Φ ≤ Σ_ops effectivePressure(op) ≤ totalOps × (valueCount + 1) bounds the split count, so
	// this cannot be reached by correct convergence — it converts a hypothetical non-terminating
	// splitter bug into a loud panic rather than a hang. (`valueCount` grows as fresh reload ids are
	// minted, but the split count is bounded by the INITIAL Φ, which the initial figures bound.)
	let iterationBound = liveness.useIndex.totalOps * (liveness.valueCount + 1) + RegisterFileSize
	var iterations = 0 as ByteCount

	// `analyzePressure` finds the worst OVER-POOL point — an op whose effective pressure exceeds
	// its OWN pool (the full pool for a normal op, the reduced `pool ∖ implicitDefs` at a fixed-
	// register point), with the same reuse-transient + dead-phi corrections `maxPressure` carries
	// so the peak-finder and this driver agree. `peak.found == false` means NO point overflows —
	// the colorer will succeed. It is recomputed each iteration because a prior spill lowers a
	// later def's live-after (an earlier-stored sibling frees a register before this def).
	var analysis = analyzePressure(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool)
	while analysis.peak.found 'relievePressure'
		if iterations >= iterationBound 'runaway'
			panic("splitLiveRanges: '{func.name}' did not converge after {iterations} splits (peak effective pressure {analysis.peak.pressure} still over its pool) — a splitter bug")
		end 'runaway'
		iterations = iterations + 1

		let peak = analysis.peak

		let index = liveness.useIndex
		let choice = chooseVictim(module, index: index, liveness: liveness, peak: peak, defLiveAfter: analysis.defLiveAfter, pool: pool, poolSize: poolSize)
		match choice 'act'
			none then try noVictimAtPeak(project, module: module, func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, liveness: liveness, peak: peak, pool: pool, lineage: lineage)
			remat(v, scope) then nextValueId = rematerializeConstant(module, index: index, topo: cfg.topo, valueId: v, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq, scope: scope, nextValueId: nextValueId, stats: stats, lineage: lineage)
			spill(v, scope) then nextValueId = spillOneValue(module, index: index, cfg: cfg, valueId: v, peak: peak, scope: scope, slot: slotForSpillOf(index, valueId: v, nextSlot: nextSlot), nextValueId: nextValueId, stats: stats, lineage: lineage)
		end 'act'
		// Only a FIRST spill of a value consumes a slot: a value relieved again at a second peak
		// reloads out of the slot its def-anchored store already wrote (see `slotForSpillOf`). `index`
		// is the PRE-split snapshot, so `hasSpillStore` still answers about the state the choice was
		// made in — the store this iteration may have just inserted is invisible to it.
		match choice 'countSlot'
			spill(v, _) then nextSlot = nextSlot if index.hasSpillStore(v) else nextSlot + 1
			none to remat then break 'countSlot'
		end 'countSlot'

		// The mutators minted fresh value ids beginning at `nextValueId` and returned the next free
		// one; every such id is DEFINED by an op they inserted, so it is exactly what a rescan of the
		// function would report as the new value count. Publishing it on the (reused) CFG is what
		// re-sizes every value-indexed column of the next liveness + index build.
		cfg.valueCount = nextValueId
		liveness = timedLiveness(module, cfg: cfg, func: func, stats: stats)
		analysis = analyzePressure(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool)
	end 'relievePressure'

	func.stackSize = nextSlot * SpillSlotBytes

	// Post-condition (design Phase 4.6): after splitting, the colorer CANNOT fail because NO
	// program point overflows its pool. The loop exits precisely on `not analysis.peak.found`,
	// which is that guarantee for BOTH the full pool (normal ops) and every reduced pool
	// (fixed-register points). `liveness.maxPressure` is an INDEPENDENT cross-check of the
	// full-pool half — it comes from TargetLiveness, a different computation than
	// `analyzePressure` — so a full-pool overflow the peak-finder somehow missed still fails
	// here rather than as a colorer panic. (The reduced-pool half has no separate maxPressure
	// analogue; the loop's `analyzePressure`-based exit is its guarantee.)
	if liveness.maxPressure > poolSize 'residual'
		panic("splitLiveRanges: '{func.name}' finished with maxPressure {liveness.maxPressure} still > pool {poolSize} — the splitter's post-condition (every point within its pool) was violated")
	end 'residual'

	return liveness
end 'splitLiveRanges'

// Nothing crossing the peak can be relieved. What that MEANS depends on which overflow it is —
// and keeping the two apart is the whole point of the amended Rule 2.
//
//  • FULL-POOL overflow → E5001, and this is the diagnostic the design exists to raise. Every
//    value crossing the point is used inside the loop, so it is the loop's genuine working set,
//    and it is larger than the machine. No spiller can fix that; only the author can, by
//    restructuring. `maxlive` is EXACT here (SSA interference is chordal, so χ = ω), so the
//    deficit is a theorem about the program, not the outcome of a search.
//
//  • CONFINED (clobber-only) overflow → a SPLITTER BUG, and it must NEVER reach the user as E5001.
//    The values confined to the witness FIT the pool; they are excluded only from the registers the
//    ops they are live across clobber. A forced bracket is therefore ALWAYS available for at least
//    one of them, and the panic below states the exact invariant that makes that so. Raising E5001
//    instead would be the false positive this design treats as its worst possible bug: it would tell
//    the author to delete a value from code that fits the machine, and the rewrite it suggests would
//    emit strictly worse code.
//
// THE INVARIANT THE CONFINED PANIC ASSERTS, in full, because "it cannot happen" was once asserted
// here on a claim that was merely NECESSARY (a store anchor) and not SUFFICIENT — and the panic was
// reachable from ordinary Maxon (`register-spill.loop-invariant-read-across-a-call-in-a-loop`):
//
//  1. EVERY value the function defines has a store anchor — an op def or a phi's block entry
//     (`buildUseIndex` gives every Target value one or the other), so `hasStoreAnchor` never refuses
//     a live value.
//  2. EVERY value's store FITS the pool. A FULL-pool overflow outranks every confined one
//     (`peakOutranks`), so by the time a confined peak is the peak, NO program point exceeds the full
//     pool — including every def point, which is what `storeFitsPool` measures. And a value already
//     in a slot needs no new store at all.
//  3. EVERY value can be made DEAD across the peak. Rewriting its after-peak uses to reloads does it
//     whenever no before-peak use is still reachable from the peak; when one IS (a loop-invariant read
//     before AND after a call inside the loop, alive across it around the back edge), rewriting EVERY
//     use does it, because the original's only remaining reader is then its own store
//     (`SplitScope.everyUse`). There is no third case: a value with no use at all is not live here.
//  4. So the ONLY confined values `chooseVictim` may refuse are the ones the peak op itself READS —
//     and it cannot refuse them all. The tight set OUTNUMBERS the witness, and any witness a clobber
//     produces has at least the five callee-saved registers in it, so at least six values are confined
//     here; an op names at most two virtual registers. At least four candidates always remain.
function noVictimAtPeak(project Project, module TargetModule, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, pool RegMask, lineage SplitLineage) throws CompileError
	if peak.clobberOnly 'splitterBug'
		panic("noVictimAtPeak: '{func.name}' has a CONFINED overflow ({peak.pressure} values confined to the {regMaskPopcount(peak.witness)} registers of witness {peak.witness}, which the full pool of {regMaskPopcount(pool)} would fit) yet `chooseVictim` refused every one of them. The invariant that must hold: a confined value the peak op does not itself read is ALWAYS relievable — it has a store anchor (an op def or a phi block entry), its store fits the pool (full-pool peaks are relieved FIRST, so no def point is over-pool by now), and reloading its after-peak uses — or, when a before-peak use is still reachable from the peak around a back edge, EVERY use — makes it dead across the peak. The tight set outnumbers the witness and the peak op names at most two virtual registers, so candidates always remain. Refusing them all is a SPLITTER BUG in `hasStoreAnchor` / `storeFitsPool` / `killsValueAtPeak` / `analyzePressure` — not user register pressure. It must never surface as E5001: this program fits the machine.")
	end 'splitterBug'

	try raiseRegisterPressure(project, module: module, func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, liveness: liveness, peak: peak, pool: pool, lineage: lineage)
end 'noVictimAtPeak'

// `computeLivenessOn`, attributed to the LIVENESS phase rather than to SPLITTING.
//
// The splitter owns every liveness computation (it runs one up front and one after each split, and
// hands the final result to the colorer), so without this the whole liveness pass was billed to
// `splitting` — and `liveness` read 0.00ms on every program, which is exactly the "74% of
// self-compile with ZERO sub-phase attribution" failure the design set out to avoid. On a large
// function liveness, not splitting, is the dominant cost, and the numbers said the opposite.
function timedLiveness(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, func IrFunction, stats RegAllocStats) returns LivenessResult
	let t = Clock.nowNanos()
	let liveness = computeLivenessOn(module, cfg: cfg, func: func)
	stats.addPhaseNanos(RegAllocPhase.liveness, nanos: Clock.elapsedNanos(t))
	return liveness
end 'timedLiveness'

// ==========================================================================
// The per-iteration pressure sweep (peak + def-point pressures)
// ==========================================================================

// ONE backward sweep computing both quantities a spill decision needs: the peak OVER-POOL op
// (where a victim is chosen) and, per value, the live-set population right after its def (the
// pressure its spill store would face, see `storeFitsPool`).
//
// The peak is the op whose demand most exceeds the registers actually available to it. Three things
// make this agree with the colorer rather than leaving it to panic:
//
//  1. EFFECTIVE pressure carries the SAME corrections TargetLiveness folds into `maxPressure`:
//     the reuse-copy transient (`reuseInputOutlivesOp` — a two-address input that outlives its
//     op costs one extra register for the `mov dest,input` the colorer inserts before it) and
//     the per-block dead-phi count (`deadPhiCountForBlock` — the colorer reserves a register for
//     each dead block-arg across the whole block). Without them a transient/dead-phi overflow
//     enters the driver loop yet finds no RAW peak here — a false panic.
//
//  2. HALL'S CONDITION on the per-value effective pools (`HallCondition`) decides the rest. A
//     forbidden set SHRINKS a value's pool — a value live across a call is confined to the 5
//     callee-saved registers, one live across an `idiv` to `pool ∖ {rax,rdx}` — so values at one
//     point can be shopping in differently sized register sets, and no single number decides
//     whether they fit. The test is: no register subset may have more values CONFINED to it than it
//     has registers. That subsumes the old `raw > popcount(pool ∖ op.implicitDefs)` check (at a
//     call, every live-across value is confined to the callee-saved subset, so the witness is that
//     subset and the count is the same `raw`) AND catches what that check structurally could not —
//     values confined by DIFFERENT calls, colliding at a point that is not itself a call, where the
//     nominal pool is the full 14 and nothing looked wrong. That was a colorer panic; see
//     `HallCondition`'s header.
//
//  3. The census the screen reads is maintained INCREMENTALLY (`stepCensusBackwardOverOp`), like the
//     live count, so no op walks its live set. It is skipped ENTIRELY — not merely screened out —
//     when no value in the function is confined (`EffectivePools.anyConstrained`): with no call, no
//     idiv and no argument pre-move, every allowed set IS the pool, so no subset can be tight and the
//     census would be tracking a distribution that provably cannot violate anything. That gate has to
//     be on the STEP, not just on the screen: the screen is per-op, but the census is stepped over
//     every OPERAND of every op, and a compiler's leaf functions are mostly call-free.
//
// Every quantity comes from the operand model + the liveness `forbidden` column, never a hardcoded
// opcode, so calls, `idiv`, `cqo` and argument pre-moves are handled uniformly. Mirrors
// TargetLiveness's pressure sweep.
function analyzePressure(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pool RegMask) returns PressureAnalysis
	let topo = liveness.cfg.topo
	let wordsPerRow = liveness.wordsPerRow
	let valueCount = liveness.valueCount
	var liveRow = zeroRow(wordsPerRow)
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	// Scratch reused across every block — otherwise one allocation per block, per iteration.
	var ordered = OpIndexArray.create()
	var seedBuf = ValueIdArray.create()
	let fullPoolSize = regMaskPopcount(pool)

	// The Hall machinery: one dense `value -> popcount(pool ∖ forbidden(v))` column, the running
	// census of the live set by that size, and the scratch its incremental maintenance + its (rare)
	// exact confirmation need. All allocated ONCE per sweep and reused across every block and op.
	let effPools = EffectivePools.of(liveness, pool: pool)
	let effPoolSizes = effPools.sizes
	// Nothing in this function is confined to a register subset (no call, no idiv, no argument
	// pre-move), so Hall's condition cannot fail against a proper subset of the pool and the census
	// would be bookkeeping for a question whose answer is already known. Skipping it takes the census
	// off the per-operand path entirely — see `EffectivePools`.
	let censusNeeded = effPools.anyConstrained
	var census = RegPoolCensus.create()
	var censusIds = ValueIdArray.create()
	var censusWasLive = BoolArray.create()
	var liveAtOp = ValueIdArray.create()

	var best = PressurePeak.create(false, pressure: 0, blockLocal: 0, liveBefore: zeroRow(wordsPerRow), peakSeq: 0, clobberOnly: false, witness: pool, atFixedRegisterOp: false)
	// A value with no op-def (a phi / a value never defined by an op) keeps this sentinel
	// (`valueCount + 1`, above any real count) so it is never mistaken for store-safe.
	var defLiveAfter = filledColumn(valueCount, value: valueCount + 1)
	var blockBaseSeq = 0 as ByteCount

	for i in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: i)
		// Seed the ONE dense working row from the SPARSE live-out list — O(live), not a full
		// value-space row copy per block. `clearRowAfterSweep` below returns it to zero the same way.
		liveness.seedRowFromLiveOut(i, row: liveRow, scratch: seedBuf)
		// The row's live population, maintained INCREMENTALLY across the block's ops. A popcount over
		// the whole row at every op would be O(values/64) per op — O(ops × values/64) per function,
		// and on a large function the single biggest cost left in the allocator.
		var liveCount = seedBuf.count()

		// The census tracks the SAME set as `liveRow`, so it is seeded and cleared with it.
		if censusNeeded 'seedTheCensus'
			census.reset()
			for v in seedBuf 'seedCensus'
				census.add(try effPoolSizes.get(v) otherwise panic("analyzePressure: live-out value {v} is outside the function's value space"), fullPoolSize: fullPoolSize)
			end 'seedCensus'
		end 'seedTheCensus'

		// A per-block constant added to every op's effective pressure — the colorer holds a
		// register for each dead block-arg across the WHOLE block. Read from the column the
		// liveness sweep already filled; recomputing it here meant re-sweeping the whole block and
		// heap-allocating two arrays, once per block, on every splitter iteration.
		let deadPhis = liveness.deadPhiCountOf(i)

		ordered.clear()
		collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
		let opCount = ordered.count()
		// The sweep runs BACKWARD (to fold live-out into live-before). The GLOBAL sequence number
		// must be the FORWARD layout position so it aligns with `UseIndex`'s numbering (which is
		// forward): op at reverse-index `ri` sits at forward position `opCount - 1 - ri`.
		for ri in 0 upto opCount 'eachOpRev'
			let forwardPos = opCount - 1 - ri
			let opIdx = try ordered.get(forwardPos) otherwise panic("analyzePressure: ordered.get OOB")
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("analyzePressure: opIdx not in module.ops")
			targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)

			// `liveRow` here is this op's live-AFTER set (the store point for anything it defines), and
			// `liveCount` is its population — no popcount needed.
			recordDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter, buf: buf, afterCount: liveCount)

			// The reuse-copy transient is decided on the live-AFTER set — BEFORE the backward step
			// re-adds this op's uses — exactly as TargetLiveness.stepBackwardOverOp reads it.
			let reuseTransient = reuseInputOutlivesOp(buf, liveRow: liveRow)

			if censusNeeded 'stepWithCensus'
				liveCount = stepCensusBackwardOverOp(liveRow, buf: buf, liveCount: liveCount, census: census, effPoolSizes: effPoolSizes, fullPoolSize: fullPoolSize, ids: censusIds, wasLive: censusWasLive)
			end 'stepWithCensus' else 'stepPlain'
				liveCount = stepRowBackwardOverOperands(liveRow, base: 0, buf: buf, liveCount: liveCount)
			end 'stepPlain'

			let raw = liveCount
			let effective = raw + (1 if reuseTransient else 0) + deadPhis
			let seq = blockBaseSeq + forwardPos

			// TWO overflows, counting TWO different demands. Lumping them over-counts the confined
			// case — it would over-spill at every call and inflate an E5001 deficit.
			//
			//  • FULL-POOL demand is `effective`: every register the colorer holds at this point,
			//    INCLUDING the dead-phi reservations and the reuse-copy transient. A dead phi is not
			//    live and a transient is not a value, so neither is CONFINED by anything — they
			//    compete for the machine, never for the callee-saved subset — which is exactly why
			//    they belong here and nowhere else. Every value is confined to the pool, so the
			//    witness is the pool itself and the overflow is plain pigeonhole: no Hall search is
			//    needed, and none is run.
			//
			//  • CONFINED demand is Hall's, over the LIVE values only, against a proper subset of the
			//    pool. Checked only when the point fits the full pool — which also bounds the live set
			//    at `poolSize`, so the exact confirmation below can never be a hot-path cost.
			//
			// A FULL-pool overflow outranks any confined one, so full-pool peaks are always relieved
			// FIRST. That ordering is what makes `storeFitsPool` sound at a confined peak: by the time
			// one is reached, no program point exceeds the full pool, so every value's def-point
			// pressure fits and a store anchor is always available.
			let atFixedRegisterOp = opIsFixedRegisterPoint(op)
			if effective > fullPoolSize 'fullPoolOverflow'
				best = betterPeak(best, pressure: effective, blockLocal: i, liveBefore: liveRow, peakSeq: seq, clobberOnly: false, witness: pool, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow)
			end 'fullPoolOverflow' else if censusNeeded and census.screenViolates(fullPoolSize) 'maybeConfined'
				bitsetCollectRow(liveRow, base: 0, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow, out: liveAtOp)
				best = confinedPeakAt(liveness, live: liveAtOp, pool: pool, best: best, blockLocal: i, liveBefore: liveRow, peakSeq: seq, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow)
			end 'maybeConfined'
		end 'eachOpRev'

		// A PHI has no defining op, so the sweep above never recorded a def-point pressure for it.
		// Its def IS this block's ENTRY (block-args arrive in their registers via the edge copies SSA
		// destruction places at the predecessors' ends), so its store anchors at the top of the block
		// and the pressure that store faces is the block's LIVE-IN population — which `liveRow` now
		// holds, the backward sweep having folded every op back to the live-before-first-op view.
		//
		// Without this a phi keeps the "no op def" sentinel (`valueCount + 1`), `storeFitsPool`
		// rejects it forever, and NO loop-carried accumulator could ever be spilled at a call — which
		// is precisely the false E5001 the amended Rule 2 exists to eliminate (`sum = sum + f(i)`
		// makes `sum` a phi). `noVictimAtPeak`'s clobber-only panic is what caught this.
		recordPhiDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter, block: block, liveInCount: liveCount)

		// Return the working row to zero for the next block, in O(live) — see clearRowAfterSweep.
		liveness.clearRowAfterSweep(i, block: block, row: liveRow, scratch: seedBuf)

		blockBaseSeq = blockBaseSeq + opCount
	end 'eachBlock'

	return PressureAnalysis.create(best, defLiveAfter: defLiveAfter)
end 'analyzePressure'

// The better of `best` and a CONFINED overflow at this op — but only if there IS one. The
// cardinality screen that got us here NEVER MISSES a real violation and is not exact about the rest
// (the forbidden masks are not laminar — see `HallCondition`), so the exact verdict decides. A false
// peak here would spill a value the program could have coloured, which is the same class of harm as
// a false E5001 in a smaller key: pressure relief added to code that was already fine.
function confinedPeakAt(liveness LivenessResult, live ValueIdArray, pool RegMask, best PressurePeak, blockLocal CfgIndex, liveBefore BitWordArray, peakSeq ByteCount, atFixedRegisterOp bool, wordsPerRow ByteCount) returns PressurePeak
	return match hallVerdictAt(liveness, live: live, pool: pool) 'verdict'
		feasible gives best
		overflow(witness, tight) gives betterPeak(best, pressure: tight, blockLocal: blockLocal, liveBefore: liveBefore, peakSeq: peakSeq, clobberOnly: true, witness: witness, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow)
	end 'verdict'
end 'confinedPeakAt'

// `best`, replaced by the candidate peak iff the candidate outranks it. The ONE place a
// `PressurePeak` is minted from a candidate, so both overflow kinds agree on the ranking and on
// snapshotting the live row (which only the WINNER pays for — `copyOfRow` is a full row copy, and
// doing it before the rank test would cost one per overflowing op rather than one per new peak).
function betterPeak(best PressurePeak, pressure RegCount, blockLocal CfgIndex, liveBefore BitWordArray, peakSeq ByteCount, clobberOnly bool, witness RegMask, atFixedRegisterOp bool, wordsPerRow ByteCount) returns PressurePeak
	if not peakOutranks(not clobberOnly, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp, pressure: pressure, best: best) 'keepBest'
		return best
	end 'keepBest'
	return PressurePeak.create(true, pressure: pressure, blockLocal: blockLocal, liveBefore: copyOfRow(liveBefore, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow), peakSeq: peakSeq, clobberOnly: clobberOnly, witness: witness, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp)
end 'betterPeak'

// Is `op` itself a FIXED-REGISTER point — does it clobber registers implicitly (a call's 9
// caller-saved, an `idiv`'s RAX/RDX)? Read off the operand model's metadata, so a new fixed-register
// op needs no edit here.
function opIsFixedRegisterPoint(op TargetOp) returns bool
	return op.rawValue.implicitDefs != 0
end 'opIsFixedRegisterPoint'

// Does a candidate overflow outrank the best peak so far?
//
// A FULL-pool overflow always outranks a CONFINED (clobber-only) one, whatever their pressures —
// which is what guarantees full-pool peaks are relieved FIRST (see the caller). Within a class, the
// higher pressure wins.
//
// At EQUAL pressure a confined candidate AT A FIXED-REGISTER OP outranks one at a normal op, and
// that tie-break is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. A value confined by a call is confined
// EVERYWHERE it is live, not just at the call — so Hall's condition sees the same violation at every
// op the confined values span, including the loop header's `cmp`. Relieving it at the CALL is the
// minimal repair: the value keeps its register at every other point, and the store/reload bracket
// sits against the op whose own clobber mask created the confinement — the placement the ABI forces,
// and the one it pays for. Without this preference the peak would drift to the earliest op the
// values happen to be live at, widening the bracket for no gain (and moving every forced-spill
// program's code for no reason). A violation visible ONLY at normal ops — values confined by
// DIFFERENT calls, converging where none of them is — has no such op, and is relieved where it is
// seen. That is the case this whole test exists to find.
//
// Otherwise ties resolve toward the EARLIER op: the sweep visits ops in DECREASING forward sequence,
// so the last update at a given pressure is the earliest one.
function peakOutranks(fullOverflow bool, atFixedRegisterOp bool, pressure RegCount, best PressurePeak) returns bool
	if not best.found 'firstPeak'
		return true
	end 'firstPeak'

	let bestIsFullPool = not best.clobberOnly
	if fullOverflow != bestIsFullPool 'differentClass'
		// The full-pool candidate wins, whichever side it is on.
		return fullOverflow
	end 'differentClass'

	if pressure != best.pressure 'byPressure'
		return pressure > best.pressure
	end 'byPressure'

	if (not fullOverflow) and atFixedRegisterOp != best.atFixedRegisterOp 'byConfiningOp'
		return atFixedRegisterOp
	end 'byConfiningOp'

	return true
end 'peakOutranks'

// Record each block-arg's def-point live count — the block's live-in population (see the caller).
function recordPhiDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, block IrBlock, liveInCount RegCount)
	for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachPhi'
		try defLiveAfter.set(arg.valueId, value: liveInCount) otherwise panic("recordPhiDefLiveAfter: defLiveAfter.set OOB for phi {arg.valueId}")
	end 'eachPhi'
end 'recordPhiDefLiveAfter'

// Record each of the op's virtual defs as living-after at `afterCount` (its store point). `buf`
// already holds the op's operands.
function recordDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, buf OperandList, afterCount RegCount)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if operandIsDef(o) and operandIsVirtual(o) 'def'
			try defLiveAfter.set(operandValueId(o), value: afterCount) otherwise panic("recordDefLiveAfter: set OOB")
		end 'def'
	end 'eachOperand'
end 'recordDefLiveAfter'

function zeroRow(wordsPerRow ByteCount) returns BitWordArray
	var row = BitWordArray.create()
	row.resize(wordsPerRow)
	return row
end 'zeroRow'

function copyOfRow(src BitWordArray, wordsPerRow ByteCount) returns BitWordArray
	var row = zeroRow(wordsPerRow)
	bitsetCopyRow(row, dstBase: 0, src: src, srcBase: 0, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow)
	return row
end 'copyOfRow'

// ==========================================================================
// Choosing a victim (Belady/MIN, with remat preferred)
// ==========================================================================

// Choose the value to relieve at the peak, and the SCOPE its split must have. Candidates are the
// values CROSSING the peak op (live-before it, minus the values the peak op itself uses — those are
// needed in a register there) that the peak's witness CONFINES. `none` when no candidate qualifies →
// at a FULL-pool peak that is the HOT overflow (E5001, `raiseRegisterPressure`); at a CONFINED one it
// is a splitter bug (`noVictimAtPeak`).
//
// FOUR TIERS, IN STRICT ORDER — and the order is the whole cost model:
//
//  1. REMAT of a constant that the eviction-point split KILLS. Free: re-emitting `mov r, imm` before
//     each after-peak use costs no store, no slot and no load.
//  2. THE EVICTION-POINT SPLIT (`SplitScope.afterPeakUses`) of a value not yet in a slot. One store at
//     the def, one reload per after-peak use-block; the before-peak uses keep the original in its
//     register for free. This is the tier every peak, cold or confined, is relieved by whenever it is
//     non-empty — which is every program that compiled before tiers 3 and 4 existed, so adding them
//     cannot change a single instruction of code that already built.
//  3. FULL REMAT (`SplitScope.everyUse`) — CONFINED peaks only. A constant read both before and after
//     a call inside a loop is live across the call around the BACK EDGE, so tier 1 cannot kill it; but
//     re-emitting it before EVERY use can, and that is still free. Preferred over any spill for the
//     same reason tier 1 is.
//  4. THE FULL SPLIT (`SplitScope.everyUse`), or a RE-RELIEF of a value already in a slot — CONFINED
//     peaks only. This is the forced bracket the amended Rule 2 owes a loop-invariant that a call
//     inside the loop reads on both sides of itself: it fits the machine (14 registers, 6 values), the
//     ABI merely denies it the 9 caller-saved ones, and the only placement that works is a load before
//     each use. Nothing is searched. See `SplitScope` and `noVictimAtPeak`.
//
// Tiers 3 and 4 are CONFINED-only because a FULL-pool peak that tier 2 cannot relieve is precisely the
// program E5001 exists to refuse: the loop's genuine working set is larger than the machine, and
// reloading at every use inside the loop is the per-iteration cost this design will not silently pay.
//
// Within a tier the victim is the one with the FARTHEST next use (Belady/MIN). A spill additionally
// needs its store to FIT the pool (`storeFitsPool`, `defLiveAfter[v] ≤ pool`): a value whose def-point
// pressure exceeds the pool has no register-free anchor for its store YET — a spilled sibling must
// free a register first, which a later iteration does. A remat has no store, and neither does the
// re-relief of a value already in a slot, so neither carries that constraint.
//
// The candidates are the SET BITS of the peak's live row, walked with `bitsetCollectRow` (which
// yields them in ascending id order, so the strict-improvement tie-break below still resolves ties
// to the lowest id). Iterating `0 upto valueCount` instead — testing each id for liveness — was
// O(values) per split for an answer of size O(pressure), and every eligibility question it then
// asked walked the whole function. That is what the `UseIndex` retires.
function chooseVictim(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, pool RegMask, poolSize RegCount) returns SpillChoice
	let topo = liveness.cfg.topo
	let placement = placementOf(peak)
	var usedAtPeak = usesAtPeakOp(module, index: index, liveness: liveness, peak: peak)
	var candidates = ValueIdArray.create()
	bitsetCollectRow(peak.liveBefore, base: 0, wordsPerRow: liveness.wordsPerRow, out: candidates)

	// `killsValueAtPeak` walks the CFG forward from the peak, stopping at the candidate's def
	// block — so the walk depends only on that def block, and the candidates crossing one peak
	// overwhelmingly share it (a loop's accumulators are all phis of the same header). Memoized
	// per def block, the whole `chooseVictim` call costs a handful of walks instead of one per
	// candidate.
	var reach = ReachCache.create(topo.numBlocks)

	var bestRemat = BeladyBest.create()
	var bestSpill = BeladyBest.create()
	var bestFullRemat = BeladyBest.create()
	var bestBracket = BeladyBest.create()

	for v in candidates 'eachValue'
		if bitsetTest(usedAtPeak, base: 0, bit: v) 'consumedHere'
			continue
		end 'consumedHere'
		if not valueConfinedTo(liveness, valueId: v, pool: pool, witness: peak.witness) 'notConfinedByThisPeak'
			// Only a value the overflow CONFINES can relieve it. At a CONFINED peak the witness is a
			// proper subset of the pool (the 5 callee-saved registers at a call), and spilling a value
			// that could have taken a register OUTSIDE it frees a register the confined values were
			// never able to use — the peak survives, the potential Φ does not fall, and the driver
			// re-picks forever. At a FULL-POOL peak the witness IS the pool, so every live value is
			// confined to it and this gate is a no-op: the E5001 path chooses from exactly the same
			// candidates it always did.
			continue
		end 'notConfinedByThisPeak'

		let nu = nextUseAfterPeak(index, valueId: v, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq)
		if isRematVictim(module, index: index, topo: topo, valueId: v, peak: peak, reach: reach) 'const'
			bestRemat.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'const' else if isSpillVictim(index, topo: topo, valueId: v, peak: peak, placement: placement, defLiveAfter: defLiveAfter, poolSize: poolSize, reach: reach) 'spillable'
			bestSpill.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'spillable' else if isFullRematVictim(module, index: index, valueId: v, placement: placement) 'fullConst'
			bestFullRemat.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'fullConst' else if isForcedBracketVictim(index, valueId: v, placement: placement, defLiveAfter: defLiveAfter, poolSize: poolSize) 'bracketable'
			bestBracket.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'bracketable'
	end 'eachValue'

	if bestRemat.found 'haveRemat'
		return SpillChoice.remat(bestRemat.valueId, scope: SplitScope.afterPeakUses)
	end 'haveRemat'
	if bestSpill.found 'haveSpill'
		return SpillChoice.spill(bestSpill.valueId, scope: SplitScope.afterPeakUses)
	end 'haveSpill'
	if bestFullRemat.found 'haveFullRemat'
		return SpillChoice.remat(bestFullRemat.valueId, scope: SplitScope.everyUse)
	end 'haveFullRemat'
	if bestBracket.found 'haveBracket'
		// The scope is resolved for the WINNER alone: `killsValueAtPeak` is the one eligibility question
		// tier 4 does not need to ask to know the value is relievable (it is relievable either way), so
		// asking it per candidate would be a walk bought for nothing.
		return SpillChoice.spill(bestBracket.valueId, scope: splitScopeAt(index, topo: topo, valueId: bestBracket.valueId, peak: peak, reach: reach))
	end 'haveBracket'
	return SpillChoice.none
end 'chooseVictim'

// The best victim offered to ONE tier so far, under Belady/MIN. Four tiers each run this same
// tournament, so it is one type rather than four copies of an `id`/`nextUse`/"is it set?" triple —
// and `found` is a field rather than an `id == valueCount` sentinel, which the tiers previously each
// had to spell out (and which only works while `valueCount` happens to be in scope).
type BeladyBest
	export var found as bool
	export var valueId as ValueId
	var farthestNextUse as ByteCount

	export static function create() returns BeladyBest
		return Self{found: false, valueId: 0, farthestNextUse: 0}
	end 'create'

	// Offer a candidate. It wins iff it is the first, or its next use is STRICTLY farther away —
	// strict, and `chooseVictim` offers candidates in ascending id order, so ties resolve to the
	// LOWEST value id and the choice is deterministic.
	export function offer(candidate ValueId, nextUse ByteCount)
		if self.found and nextUse <= self.farthestNextUse 'noImprovement'
			return
		end 'noImprovement'

		self.found = true
		self.valueId = candidate
		self.farthestNextUse = nextUse
	end 'offer'
end 'BeladyBest'

// The global sequence number of `valueId`'s FIRST OP use strictly after `peakSeq` (Belady's
// next-use distance from the peak), or the `totalOps` sentinel for "no later use".
//
// EDGE uses are deliberately NOT counted — an edge arg is a use the SPLIT must rewrite, but it is
// not a next-use DISTANCE the eviction ranks on, and the forward sweep over op OPERANDS this
// replaces did not see them either.
//
// The sentinel is `totalOps`, which is strictly greater than every real sequence number (0 ..
// totalOps-1), so a value never used again sorts as the FARTHEST next use — the best Belady
// candidate — while still comparing correctly under `>`. (A `u64.max` sentinel does NOT: on
// `int(0 to u64.max)` its all-ones pattern compares as negative under `>`, so Belady would collapse
// to first-fit and evict the lowest id rather than the farthest next use.)
//
// The value's use entries are in ascending sequence order (the CSR was filled in layout order), so
// the FIRST one past the peak is the nearest — O(uses of this one value), never a sweep.
function nextUseAfterPeak(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, peakSeq ByteCount) returns ByteCount
	for j in 0 upto index.useCount(valueId) 'each'
		let record = index.useRecordAt(valueId, k: j)
		match index.useKindAt(record) 'kind'
			edge then continue
			op then break 'kind'
		end 'kind'
		let seq = index.useSeqAt(record)
		if seq > peakSeq 'later'
			return seq
		end 'later'
	end 'each'
	return index.totalOps
end 'nextUseAfterPeak'

// True iff `valueId`'s def-point live-set fits the pool. The store ALWAYS anchors at the def (that
// is the only point that dominates every reload — see the "STORE ANCHOR IS THE VALUE'S DEF" note),
// and `defLiveAfter[v]` is the pressure exactly there, so `defLiveAfter[v] ≤ pool` IS "the store has
// a register-free anchor". A value whose def point is still over-pool has no anchor YET: a spilled
// sibling must free a register there first, which a later iteration does (a FULL-pool peak always
// outranks a confined one, so by the time a confined peak is reached no point is over-pool at all and
// this gate cannot refuse anything).
//
// A value already IN a slot skips this gate entirely — its store exists and is not re-emitted.
function storeFitsPool(defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, valueId ValueId, poolSize RegCount) returns bool
	let after = try defLiveAfter.get(valueId) otherwise panic("storeFitsPool: defLiveAfter.get OOB for value {valueId}")
	return after <= poolSize
end 'storeFitsPool'

// The set of values the peak op READS (its virtual uses) — excluded from the candidate pool
// because they are needed in a register AT the peak, so spilling them across it is impossible.
//
// This exclusion can never empty the candidate pool at a CONFINED peak, which is one leg of the
// invariant `noVictimAtPeak` asserts: an op names at most TWO virtual registers, while the tight set
// outnumbers the witness and any witness a clobber produces holds at least the five callee-saved
// registers — so at least four confined values survive this cut.
function usesAtPeakOp(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak) returns BitWordArray
	var row = zeroRow(liveness.wordsPerRow)
	let op = try module.ops.get(index.opAtSequence(peak.peakSeq)) otherwise panic("usesAtPeakOp: peak op not in module.ops")
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if (not operandIsDef(o)) and operandIsVirtual(o) 'use'
			bitsetAdd(row, base: 0, bit: operandValueId(o))
		end 'use'
	end 'eachOperand'
	return row
end 'usesAtPeakOp'

// ==========================================================================
// Victim eligibility
// ==========================================================================

// Is `valueId` a TIER-1 victim — a constant the EVICTION-POINT re-emit (`SplitScope.afterPeakUses`)
// actually KILLS at the peak? Its def must be a constant materialization, AND re-emitting only its
// after-peak uses must leave it dead across the peak — the same second gate the eviction-point spill
// has, and for the same reason.
//
// `killsValueAtPeak` is not a spill-specific test: remat partitions a value's uses around the peak
// EXACTLY as a spill does (after-peak uses are rewritten to fresh copies, before-peak uses keep the
// original), so the original is dead across the peak under precisely the same condition. Without
// this gate a constant whose every use PRECEDES the peak in layout order but which is live across it
// AROUND A BACK EDGE — `let c = 7` used only in a `while i mod c < 3` header, with the loop's peak
// in the body below — is chosen, re-emits NOTHING (it has no after-peak use), drops nothing, and
// relieves nothing. The potential Φ does not decrease, the next iteration re-picks the same value,
// and the driver spins until the runaway bound panics. That is exactly the failure the spill path's
// `killsValueAtPeak` was added to prevent; the remat path simply never got it. Such a constant is not
// un-relievable — at a CONFINED peak `isFullRematVictim` re-emits it before EVERY use, which is still
// free — it merely cannot be relieved by the narrow re-emit.
//
// Neither remat tier needs a `hasSpillStore`-style already-done guard, because a rematerializable
// value is NEVER spilled: at every peak it either takes a remat tier or is refused, and the spill
// tiers sit behind them in the same `else if` chain. So it never acquires a store to guard against.
function isRematVictim(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, peak PressurePeak, reach ReachCache) returns bool
	if not isRematerializable(module, index: index, valueId: valueId) 'notConst'
		return false
	end 'notConst'
	return killsValueAtPeak(index, topo: topo, valueId: valueId, peak: peak, reach: reach)
end 'isRematVictim'

// Is `valueId` a TIER-2 victim — the eviction-point split, the cheapest spill there is? Four
// independent gates, all necessary:
//
//  1. not already stored — a value an earlier iteration already wrote to a slot needs no second
//                          store, so its relief is tier 4's (reloads only), not this one's.
//  2. `isSpillable`      — its shape suits the peak's PLACEMENT (cold needs a depth-0, non-phi,
//                          non-edge-passed value; forced needs only a store anchor).
//  3. `storeFitsPool`    — its store has a register-free anchor point.
//  4. `killsValueAtPeak` — the eviction-point split actually makes it DEAD at the peak. Without this
//                          the splitter emits stores that relieve nothing (see `killsValueAtPeak`).
//                          A value that fails HERE is not un-relievable — at a confined peak the FULL
//                          split still kills it (tier 4) — it just cannot be relieved this cheaply.
function isSpillVictim(index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, peak PressurePeak, placement SpillPlacement, defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, poolSize RegCount, reach ReachCache) returns bool
	if index.hasSpillStore(valueId) 'alreadyStored'
		return false
	end 'alreadyStored'
	if not isSpillable(index, valueId: valueId, placement: placement) 'wrongShape'
		return false
	end 'wrongShape'
	if not storeFitsPool(defLiveAfter, valueId: valueId, poolSize: poolSize) 'noStoreAnchor'
		return false
	end 'noStoreAnchor'
	return killsValueAtPeak(index, topo: topo, valueId: valueId, peak: peak, reach: reach)
end 'isSpillVictim'

// Is `valueId` a TIER-3 victim — a constant the eviction-point split could not kill, but re-emitting
// at EVERY use can? Still free (no store, no slot, no load), so it outranks every spill.
//
// CONFINED peaks only. At a FULL-pool peak a constant the loop reads every iteration is part of the
// loop's genuine working set, and rescuing it here would silently move the E5001 cliff — the line this
// design draws between "the placement is forced" and "the machine is too small" (see `chooseVictim`).
function isFullRematVictim(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, placement SpillPlacement) returns bool
	return isForcedPlacement(placement) and isRematerializable(module, index: index, valueId: valueId)
end 'isFullRematVictim'

// Is `valueId` a TIER-4 victim — relievable by the FORCED bracket, whatever `killsValueAtPeak` says?
//
// This is the tier that makes `noVictimAtPeak`'s confined panic unreachable, and it is deliberately
// TOTAL over the confined values: given a store anchor and somewhere to put the store, EVERY value can
// be made dead across the peak (`splitScopeAt` picks how). It refuses only what it structurally must:
//
//  • no store anchor — impossible for a value this function defines, but `hasStoreAnchor` is the one
//    place that is stated, so it is asked rather than assumed;
//  • a NEW store that does not fit the pool — a value already in a slot needs no new store at all, so
//    that gate applies only to a first spill.
//
// CONFINED peaks only, for the same reason tier 3 is: at a FULL-pool peak, reloading a value the loop
// uses at every one of its uses is exactly the per-iteration cost E5001 refuses to pay silently.
function isForcedBracketVictim(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, placement SpillPlacement, defLiveAfter CfgIndexArray, poolSize RegCount) returns bool
	if not isForcedPlacement(placement) 'coldPeak'
		return false
	end 'coldPeak'
	if not hasStoreAnchor(index, valueId: valueId) 'noAnchor'
		return false
	end 'noAnchor'
	if index.hasSpillStore(valueId) 'alreadyInASlot'
		return true
	end 'alreadyInASlot'
	return storeFitsPool(defLiveAfter, valueId: valueId, poolSize: poolSize)
end 'isForcedBracketVictim'

// The narrowest split scope that leaves `valueId` DEAD across the peak: the eviction-point split when
// it suffices, the full split when a before-peak use is still reachable from the peak (see
// `killsValueAtPeak` and `SplitScope`).
function splitScopeAt(index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, peak PressurePeak, reach ReachCache) returns SplitScope
	return SplitScope.afterPeakUses if killsValueAtPeak(index, topo: topo, valueId: valueId, peak: peak, reach: reach) else SplitScope.everyUse
end 'splitScopeAt'

function isForcedPlacement(placement SpillPlacement) returns bool
	return match placement 'p'
		cold gives false
		forced gives true
	end 'p'
end 'isForcedPlacement'

// The placement a peak's relief is entitled to: a clobber-only overflow gets the FORCED
// store/reload bracket (any loop depth); a full-pool overflow gets only a COLD split.
function placementOf(peak PressurePeak) returns SpillPlacement
	return SpillPlacement.forced if peak.clobberOnly else SpillPlacement.cold
end 'placementOf'

// Can `valueId` be relieved at a peak of this kind? The two kinds have DIFFERENT eligibility, and
// that difference IS the amended Rule 2 (see `SpillPlacement`).
function isSpillable(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, placement SpillPlacement) returns bool
	return match placement 'p'
		cold gives isColdSpillable(index, valueId: valueId)
		forced gives hasStoreAnchor(index, valueId: valueId)
	end 'p'
end 'isSpillable'

// A value is COLD-spillable iff its def AND every use are at loop depth 0, it is not a phi,
// and it is not passed on any branch edge. That exact shape is what a COLD spill needs: the store
// (after a depth-0 def or before-peak use) and every reload (before a depth-0 after-peak use)
// then lie OUTSIDE every loop, so no spill code is added to a loop body. A value USED inside a
// loop is deliberately excluded HERE — at a FULL-pool overflow it is the loop's genuine working
// set, and if that exceeds the machine the answer is E5001, not a reload at every use.
//
// NOTE this is the FULL-POOL gate only. A fixed-register (clobber-only) peak uses
// `hasStoreAnchor` instead: there the value is NOT over the machine's capacity, so refusing to
// spill it would be a false E5001 (see `PressurePeak.clobberOnly`).
function isColdSpillable(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	if index.isPhiValue(valueId) 'phi'
		return false
	end 'phi'
	if index.isEdgePassed(valueId) 'edge'
		return false
	end 'edge'
	return index.defAndUsesAtDepthZero(valueId)
end 'isColdSpillable'

// A value can anchor a spill STORE iff it has a point at which it is already in a register and
// which precedes the peak: an OP DEF (store right after it, or after its last pre-peak use), or a
// PHI (store at the very top of its own block — a block-arg arrives in its register via the edge
// copies SSA destruction places at the predecessors' ends, so it is live at block entry, and its
// block dominates every use of it).
//
// This is the SHAPE test for a FORCED (clobber-only) spill — no loop-depth bar, no phi bar, no
// edge-passed bar. Phis and edge-passed values are exactly the loop-carried accumulators that
// dominate the live-across-a-call set in real code (`sum = sum + f(i)` makes `sum` a phi and its
// update a back-edge arg), so excluding them would leave nothing to spill and turn every such loop
// into a false E5001. `spillOneValue` rewrites branch-edge args alongside op uses, which is what
// makes the edge-passed case sound.
//
// Every value a function DEFINES has one of the two anchors (`buildUseIndex` records an op def or a
// phi block for each), so this never refuses a value that is live at a peak — which is one of the
// four legs of the invariant `noVictimAtPeak` asserts.
function hasStoreAnchor(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	return match index.defSiteOf(valueId) 'site'
		none gives false
		opDef to phiDef gives true
	end 'site'
end 'hasStoreAnchor'

// A value is REMATERIALIZABLE iff its single def is an integer-constant materialization
// (`movRegImm`/`movRegImm32`). Such a value is re-emitted at each use (never spilled), so —
// unlike a spill — it needs no depth-0 restriction on its uses; but it must still not be a phi
// or edge-passed value (those have no op-use site to re-emit before), so those are excluded.
function isRematerializable(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	if index.isPhiValue(valueId) 'phi'
		return false
	end 'phi'
	if index.isEdgePassed(valueId) 'edge'
		return false
	end 'edge'
	match index.defSiteOf(valueId) 'def'
		opDef(opIdx, _) then return opIsConstMaterialization(module, opIdx: opIdx)
		none then return false
		phiDef then return false
	end 'def'
end 'isRematerializable'

function opIsConstMaterialization(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("opIsConstMaterialization: opIdx not in module.ops")
	return match op 'k'
		movRegImm gives true
		movRegImm32 gives true
		prologue to jcc gives false
		movRegReg to idivReg gives false
		pushReg to loadRegSlot gives false
	end 'k'
end 'opIsConstMaterialization'

// Would splitting `valueId` at the peak actually KILL it there? Splitting rewrites the uses AFTER
// the peak to reloads and leaves the uses BEFORE it reading the original — so the value is dead
// across the peak only if no BEFORE-peak use is still reachable FROM the peak. Under a back edge
// it often is, and then the spill relieves nothing.
//
// The loop bound in `while i < n` is the case that exposed this: `n`'s only use is the header's
// compare, which sits BEFORE the loop's call in layout order — so the splitter would store `n` and
// emit NO reload, while `n` stays live across the call via the back edge. A wasted slot, a wasted
// store in the loop header every iteration, and ZERO pressure relief. (It converged only because
// `hasSpillStore` refuses to store the same id twice, which is an accident, not the termination
// argument: the potential Φ did not decrease that iteration.)
//
// Reachability must respect SSA. A value has exactly ONE def, so if a before-peak use is reachable
// from the peak WITHOUT passing that def, the value is genuinely still live there. Passing the def
// REDEFINES it, and the use then reads the new value — which is precisely why a loop-header PHI is
// relievable while a loop-invariant is not: every back-edge path to the header re-enters the phi's
// def. So the walk below simply never expands INTO the def's block.
//
//   `n`   (def outside the loop, used in the header) → header reachable without redef → REFUSED.
//   `i`   (a header PHI, used in the header)         → header is its own def block    → allowed.
//   `sum` (a header PHI, used after the call)        → no before-peak use at all      → allowed.
function killsValueAtPeak(index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, peak PressurePeak, reach ReachCache) returns bool
	let useCount = index.useCount(valueId)

	var hasBeforePeakUse = false
	for j in 0 upto useCount 'scan'
		if index.useSeqAt(index.useRecordAt(valueId, k: j)) < peak.peakSeq 'before'
			hasBeforePeakUse = true
		end 'before'
	end 'scan'
	if not hasBeforePeakUse 'allAfter'
		// Every use follows the peak, so every one becomes a reload. The value is dead across it.
		return true
	end 'allAfter'

	let defBlock = index.defBlockOf(valueId)
	for j in 0 upto useCount 'check'
		let record = index.useRecordAt(valueId, k: j)
		if index.useSeqAt(record) >= peak.peakSeq 'afterPeak'
			continue
		end 'afterPeak'
		if reach.reachableFromPeak(topo, peakBlockLocal: peak.blockLocal, defBlock: defBlock, block: index.useBlockAt(record)) 'notKilled'
			return false
		end 'notKilled'
	end 'check'
	return true
end 'killsValueAtPeak'

// Memoized "blocks reachable from the peak without passing through a def block", keyed by that
// def block. The peak is fixed for one `chooseVictim` call, so the walk is a function of the def
// block alone — and a loop's candidates are overwhelmingly phis of the SAME header, so one walk
// serves them all. Rows live in ONE flat `rows × numBlocks` array (never an array of arrays).
type ReachCache
	var numBlocks as CfgIndex
	// defBlock -> its row index in `rows`, or `numBlocks` for "not walked yet" (no row index can
	// equal numBlocks: there are at most numBlocks distinct def blocks, so rows are 0..numBlocks-1).
	var rowOf as CfgIndexArray
	var rows as BoolArray
	var rowCount as CfgIndex

	static function create(numBlocks CfgIndex) returns ReachCache
		return Self{numBlocks: numBlocks, rowOf: filledColumn(numBlocks, value: numBlocks), rows: BoolArray.create(), rowCount: 0}
	end 'create'

	// Is `block` reachable from the peak's SUCCESSORS without passing through `defBlock`?
	export function reachableFromPeak(topo FuncTopology, peakBlockLocal CfgIndex, defBlock CfgIndex, block CfgIndex) returns bool
		let row = self.rowFor(topo, peakBlockLocal: peakBlockLocal, defBlock: defBlock)
		return try self.rows.get(row * self.numBlocks + block) otherwise panic("ReachCache.reachableFromPeak: block {block} out of range")
	end 'reachableFromPeak'

	function rowFor(topo FuncTopology, peakBlockLocal CfgIndex, defBlock CfgIndex) returns CfgIndex
		let cached = try self.rowOf.get(defBlock) otherwise panic("ReachCache.rowFor: def block {defBlock} out of range")
		if cached != self.numBlocks 'hit'
			return cached
		end 'hit'

		let row = self.rowCount
		self.rowCount = row + 1
		self.rows.resize(self.rowCount * self.numBlocks)
		markBlocksReachableFromPeak(topo, peakBlockLocal: peakBlockLocal, defBlock: defBlock, rows: self.rows, base: row * self.numBlocks)
		try self.rowOf.set(defBlock, value: row) otherwise panic("ReachCache.rowFor: rowOf.set OOB")
		return row
	end 'rowFor'
end 'ReachCache'

// Mark, into `rows[base .. base+numBlocks)`, the blocks reachable from `peakBlockLocal` WITHOUT
// passing through `defBlock` — the blocks in which the value is still carrying its pre-peak
// definition. The walk starts at the peak block's SUCCESSORS (so the peak's own block is reached
// only via a genuine cycle) and never expands into `defBlock` (entering it redefines the value;
// see `killsValueAtPeak`). Worklist-driven: no CFG traversal in the allocator may recurse.
function markBlocksReachableFromPeak(topo FuncTopology, peakBlockLocal CfgIndex, defBlock CfgIndex, rows BoolArray, base ByteCount)
	var worklist = CfgIndexArray.create()
	let deg = topo.succ.degree(peakBlockLocal)
	for k in 0 upto deg 'seed'
		worklist.push(topo.succ.neighbour(peakBlockLocal, k: k))
	end 'seed'

	while worklist.count() > 0 'bfs'
		let b = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("markBlocksReachableFromPeak: worklist.pop on non-empty failed")
		if b == defBlock 'redefined'
			continue
		end 'redefined'
		let seen = try rows.get(base + b) otherwise panic("markBlocksReachableFromPeak: rows.get OOB")
		if seen 'already'
			continue
		end 'already'
		try rows.set(base + b, value: true) otherwise panic("markBlocksReachableFromPeak: rows.set OOB")

		let d = topo.succ.degree(b)
		for k in 0 upto d 'eachSucc'
			worklist.push(topo.succ.neighbour(b, k: k))
		end 'eachSucc'
	end 'bfs'
end 'markBlocksReachableFromPeak'

// ==========================================================================
// Materializing a spill (Belady split at the eviction point)
// ==========================================================================

// THE STORE ANCHOR IS THE VALUE'S DEF, ALWAYS — because the def is the only point that is
// guaranteed to DOMINATE every reload, and a reload reading a slot its store does not dominate is a
// garbage read.
//
// The store used to be placed after the value's LAST use BEFORE the peak, on the reasoning that "it
// precedes the peak, the reloads follow it". LAYOUT ORDER IS NOT DOMINANCE, and that reasoning is
// wrong in two ways at once:
//
//  • A before-peak use can sit in a block that does NOT dominate the reload sites — the `then` arm
//    of an `if`, whose store never runs on the `else` path, leaving the slot unwritten under the
//    reload that follows the merge.
//  • A before-peak use can be an EDGE ARG, whose "op" is the block's TERMINATOR. A store cannot be
//    spliced after a terminator, and `positionInBlock` (body ops only) panicked outright. That is
//    the crash `register-spill.forced-spill-with-edge-arg-before-the-peak` pins.
//
// The def has neither problem: by SSA (Rule 1) it dominates every use of the value, hence every
// reload, and it is an op (or a phi's block entry) that a store can legally follow. It is also
// STRICTLY BETTER code: a value defined outside a loop and used inside it before the loop's peak
// would have anchored its store at that in-loop use and re-executed it every iteration; anchored at
// the def it runs ONCE. And it is what `storeFitsPool` already measures — that gate reads
// `defLiveAfter[v]`, the pressure at the DEF point, which is now exactly the point the store lands.
//
// Nothing about the SPLIT changes: only the after-peak uses are rewritten to reloads, so the value
// is still dead across the peak and the pressure there still drops by one. The store merely writes
// memory; it never kills the value, so anchoring it earlier costs no register and no pressure.

// Spill `valueId` by SPLITTING its live range AT the peak (`peak.peakSeq`), so it is DEAD across
// the peak — freeing its register there, which is what lowers peak pressure. The store goes at the
// value's DEF (after the defining op, or at the top of its block if it is a phi — see the note
// above on why the def is the ONLY sound anchor); a reload (a fresh value id) goes before the first
// AFTER-peak use in each use-block, and ONLY the after-peak uses in that block are rewritten to
// it — the before-peak uses keep the original value in its original register. The store DOMINATES
// every reload (the def dominates every use, by SSA) and each reload dominates the after-peak uses
// it serves, so no phi is ever needed (the dominating-reload invariant). Returns the next free
// value id.
//
// The peak's placement is the amended Rule 2: `cold` asserts every inserted op lands outside every
// loop; `forced` permits the store/reload bracket INSIDE a loop, which is the only way to relieve a
// fixed-register point whose live-across set exceeds the registers the op leaves alone.
//
// EDGE ARGS are rewritten alongside op uses. A branch-edge arg is a real use, read at the end of
// its block; leaving one pointing at the ORIGINAL would keep that original live to the block's end
// — hence still live across the peak — so the spill would not lower the pressure and the driver
// would re-pick the same value forever. Loop-carried accumulators are exactly this shape.
//
// The index is read ONLY through fields that survive the insertions below: an op's `module.ops`
// index (the table is append-only) and a use's owning block. Positions inside a block shift as ops
// are spliced in, and every insertion re-derives its own (see `positionInBlock`).
function spillOneValue(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId, peak PressurePeak, scope SplitScope, slot SpillSlot, nextValueId ValueId, stats RegAllocStats, lineage SplitLineage) returns ValueId
	let peakSeq = peak.peakSeq
	let placement = placementOf(peak)
	let useCount = index.useCount(valueId)
	let keepsOriginal = keepsOriginalBeforePeak(scope)

	// STORE: at the def, which dominates every reload below — and ONCE per value. An earlier split
	// already wrote this exact id to this exact slot (`slotForSpillOf` handed it back), and a second
	// store at the same def would write a value the first store already wrote, into a second slot.
	if not index.hasSpillStore(valueId) 'firstSpillOfThisValue'
		insertStore(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId, defSite: index.defSiteOf(valueId), slot: slot, placement: placement)
	end 'firstSpillOfThisValue'

	// RELOAD: one per (use-block, side-of-the-peak) RUN of the uses this split rewrites. Use sites are
	// in layout order, so a block's uses are contiguous and its before-peak ones precede its after-peak
	// ones; each boundary — a new block, or the peak falling between two uses of the SAME block —
	// starts a fresh reload id, which dominates the run it serves (it precedes the run's first use,
	// and a block's edge arg is read after every op in it).
	//
	// An `afterPeakUses` split skips the before-peak runs entirely: those uses keep the ORIGINAL value
	// in its original register, for free. An `everyUse` split rewrites them too, which is what leaves
	// the original with no reader but its own store — see `SplitScope`.
	var freshId = nextValueId
	var reloadCount = 0 as ByteCount
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	var j = 0 as ByteCount
	while j < useCount 'eachUse'
		let record = index.useRecordAt(valueId, k: j)
		let afterPeak = index.useSeqAt(record) >= peakSeq
		if (not afterPeak) and keepsOriginal 'beforePeakUseKeepsTheOriginal'
			j = j + 1
			continue
		end 'beforePeakUseKeepsTheOriginal'

		let blockLocal = index.useBlockAt(record)
		insertReloadFor(module, cfg: cfg, index: index, useRecord: record, valueId: valueId, freshId: freshId, slot: slot, placement: placement)
		// The reload id derives from the value it reloads — record it so a reload id in an
		// E5001 blocking set chases back to that value's source origin (see SplitLineage).
		lineage.record(freshId, sourceId: valueId)

		while j < useCount and useIsInRun(index, valueId: valueId, k: j, blockLocal: blockLocal, afterPeak: afterPeak, peakSeq: peakSeq) 'sameRun'
			let r = index.useRecordAt(valueId, k: j)
			match index.useKindAt(r) 'kind'
				op then rewriteOpAt(module, opIdx: index.useOpAt(r), from: valueId, to: freshId, buf: buf)
				edge then rewriteEdgeArgsIn(module, topo: cfg.topo, blockLocal: blockLocal, from: valueId, to: freshId)
			end 'kind'
			j = j + 1
		end 'sameRun'

		freshId = freshId + 1
		reloadCount = reloadCount + 1
	end 'eachUse'

	stats.valuesSplit = stats.valuesSplit + 1
	stats.reloadsInserted = stats.reloadsInserted + reloadCount
	match placement 'countForced'
		forced then stats.forcedSpills = stats.forcedSpills + 1
		cold then break 'countForced'
	end 'countForced'
	return freshId
end 'spillOneValue'

// Is `valueId`'s `k`-th use in the run one reload serves — the same block, on the same side of the
// peak? A run may NOT straddle the peak: a reload placed before a block's before-peak use would then
// serve its after-peak uses too, and that reloaded value would span the peak, so the pressure there
// would never drop. (That is the whole-range-reload failure the file header names, in the small.)
function useIsInRun(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, k ByteCount, blockLocal CfgIndex, afterPeak bool, peakSeq ByteCount) returns bool
	let record = index.useRecordAt(valueId, k: k)
	if index.useBlockAt(record) != blockLocal 'otherBlock'
		return false
	end 'otherBlock'
	return (index.useSeqAt(record) >= peakSeq) == afterPeak
end 'useIsInRun'

// The slot a spill of `valueId` writes into, or reloads out of. A value is stored ONCE, at its def:
// the def dominates every reload wherever a later peak puts one, so relieving the value AGAIN needs
// only more loads out of the slot it already has. `nextSlot` — a fresh slot — is for a value that has
// never been spilled.
function slotForSpillOf(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, nextSlot SpillSlot) returns SpillSlot
	return match index.spillSlotOf(valueId) 'existing'
		none gives nextSlot
		slot(s) gives s
	end 'existing'
end 'slotForSpillOf'

// Insert the spill STORE at the value's def — the one point that dominates every reload (see above).
function insertStore(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId, defSite ValueDefSite, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement)
	match defSite 'site'
		opDef(opIdx, blockLocal) then insertStoreAfter(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId, afterOpIdx: opIdx, blockLocal: blockLocal, slot: slot, placement: placement)
		phiDef(blockLocal) then insertStoreAtBlockEntry(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId, blockLocal: blockLocal, slot: slot, placement: placement)
		none then panic("insertStore: value {valueId} has neither an op def nor a phi block, so it has no store anchor — `hasStoreAnchor` should have rejected it as a victim")
	end 'site'
end 'insertStore'

// Insert the RELOAD serving a block's after-peak uses, anchored on the FIRST of them (use entry
// `useAt`). An OP use takes the reload immediately before it. An EDGE use means the block has no
// after-peak op use at all (op uses precede the edge use in the index), so the value is only
// forwarded to a phi — the reload goes at the END of the block's body, still ahead of the edge
// that reads it.
function insertReloadFor(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, index UseIndex, useRecord ByteCount, valueId ValueId, freshId ValueId, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement)
	let blockLocal = index.useBlockAt(useRecord)
	match index.useKindAt(useRecord) 'k'
		op then insertReloadBefore(module, cfg: cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, beforeOpIdx: index.useOpAt(useRecord), valueId: valueId, freshId: freshId, slot: slot, placement: placement)
		edge then insertReloadAtBlockEnd(module, cfg: cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, freshId: freshId, slot: slot, placement: placement)
	end 'k'
end 'insertReloadFor'

// Repoint every branch-edge arg of `blockLocal` naming `from` at `to` (the reload that now holds
// the value). The reload precedes the block's terminator, so it dominates the edge.
function rewriteEdgeArgsIn(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, blockLocal CfgIndex, from ValueId, to ValueId)
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: blockLocal)
	for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
		for k in 0 upto edge.argIds.count() 'eachArg'
			let argId = try edge.argIds.get(k) otherwise panic("rewriteEdgeArgsIn: argIds.get OOB")
			if argId == from 'match'
				try edge.argIds.set(k, value: to) otherwise panic("rewriteEdgeArgsIn: argIds.set OOB")
			end 'match'
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachEdge'
end 'rewriteEdgeArgsIn'

// Insert `storeSlotReg(slot, valueId)` right after `afterOpIdx` — the op that DEFINES the value, so
// it is live there by construction and the store reads it from its own register.
function insertStoreAfter(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId, afterOpIdx OpIndex, blockLocal CfgIndex, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement)
	assertPlacementLegal(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: "store", placement: placement)
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: cfg.topo, local: blockLocal)
	let anchorPos = positionInBlock(block, opIdx: afterOpIdx)
	try block.opRefs.insert(anchorPos + 1, value: appendOp(module, op: TargetOp.storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg: X64VReg.virtual(valueId)))) otherwise panic("insertStoreAfter: opRefs.insert OOB")
end 'insertStoreAfter'

// Insert `storeSlotReg(slot, valueId)` at the very TOP of `blockLocal` — the PHI anchor. A
// block-arg has no defining op; it arrives in its register via the edge copies SSA destruction
// places at the predecessors' ends, so it is live at block entry and a store there reads it
// correctly. Its block dominates every use of it, so this store dominates every reload.
function insertStoreAtBlockEntry(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId, blockLocal CfgIndex, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement)
	assertPlacementLegal(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: "store", placement: placement)
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: cfg.topo, local: blockLocal)
	try block.opRefs.insert(0, value: appendOp(module, op: TargetOp.storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg: X64VReg.virtual(valueId)))) otherwise panic("insertStoreAtBlockEntry: opRefs.insert OOB")
end 'insertStoreAtBlockEntry'

// Insert `loadRegSlot(freshId, slot)` right before `beforeOpIdx` (the first after-peak OP use in
// `blockLocal`). The reload dominates every after-peak use in the block (it precedes the first
// one, and the edge arg is read after them all). The caller rewrites those uses.
function insertReloadBefore(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, blockLocal CfgIndex, beforeOpIdx OpIndex, valueId ValueId, freshId ValueId, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement)
	assertPlacementLegal(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: "reload", placement: placement)
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: cfg.topo, local: blockLocal)
	let pos = positionInBlock(block, opIdx: beforeOpIdx)
	try block.opRefs.insert(pos, value: appendOp(module, op: TargetOp.loadRegSlot(X64VReg.virtual(freshId), slot: slot))) otherwise panic("insertReloadBefore: opRefs.insert OOB")
end 'insertReloadBefore'

// Insert `loadRegSlot(freshId, slot)` at the END of `blockLocal`'s body — after every op, ahead of
// the branch and so ahead of every branch edge that reads the value. Used when a block's only
// after-peak use is an EDGE arg: the value is not read by any op there, it is merely forwarded to
// a phi, so the reload exists purely to hand the edge a live register.
//
// "Ahead of the branch" is NOT the end of `opRefs` when the block branches two ways: a `jcc` is a
// BODY op paired with a `jmp` terminator, so an op past the end of the body sits BETWEEN them and
// runs only on the not-taken path — the then-edge would then read an unloaded register. The reload
// goes immediately BEFORE the conditional branch, which dominates both outgoing edges. A
// `loadRegSlot` is a plain `mov r, [rsp+disp]` and writes no flags, so it is safe between the
// compare and the `jcc` that reads that compare's EFLAGS.
function insertReloadAtBlockEnd(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, blockLocal CfgIndex, valueId ValueId, freshId ValueId, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement)
	assertPlacementLegal(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: "reload", placement: placement)
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: cfg.topo, local: blockLocal)
	let pos = beforeBranchPosition(block)
	try block.opRefs.insert(pos, value: appendOp(module, op: TargetOp.loadRegSlot(X64VReg.virtual(freshId), slot: slot))) otherwise panic("insertReloadAtBlockEnd: opRefs.insert OOB")
end 'insertReloadAtBlockEnd'

// The `opRefs` position at which an op runs after the whole body but on EVERY edge out of the
// block: past the last real body op, but before the conditional branch when the block has one.
function beforeBranchPosition(block IrBlock) returns ByteCount
	if block.hasCondBranchOp() 'twoWay'
		return positionInBlock(block, opIdx: block.condBranchIndex())
	end 'twoWay'
	return block.opRefs.count()
end 'beforeBranchPosition'

// Append `op` to the module's op table and return its index — the one place a splitter-minted op
// enters `module.ops`, so the insertion sites above cannot drift on how they mint. APPEND-ONLY is
// load-bearing: it is why every op index the `UseIndex` snapshot holds stays valid while a split
// is being materialized.
function appendOp(module TargetModule, op TargetOp) returns OpIndex
	let idx = module.ops.count()
	module.ops.push(op)
	return idx
end 'appendOp'

// ==========================================================================
// Materializing a remat (re-emit the constant before each after-peak use)
// ==========================================================================

// Rematerialize the constant `valueId` AT the peak (`peakSeq`): re-emit its `movRegImm(32)` (a
// fresh value id) right before EACH use this split rewrites, rewriting that use to the fresh copy. A
// constant is trivially rematerializable, so per-use re-emit is fine — it holds no register across
// the gap and never touches memory, so unlike a spill it needs no depth-0 restriction on its uses
// (re-emitting a constant even inside a loop is free). Returns the next free value id.
//
// The SCOPE says which uses are rewritten (see `SplitScope`). An `afterPeakUses` remat leaves the
// before-peak uses reading the ORIGINAL, so the original stays live from its def to the last of them
// — dead across the peak, which is what `killsValueAtPeak` established before choosing it. An
// `everyUse` remat rewrites all of them, which is the ONLY thing that kills a constant kept alive
// across the peak by a before-peak use AROUND A BACK EDGE. Either way the original def is dropped
// exactly when nothing reads it any more.
function rematerializeConstant(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, peakSeq ByteCount, scope SplitScope, nextValueId ValueId, stats RegAllocStats, lineage SplitLineage) returns ValueId
	let defOpIdx = index.requireDefOp(valueId)
	let constValue = constValueOfDef(module, opIdx: defOpIdx)
	let keepsOriginal = keepsOriginalBeforePeak(scope)

	var freshId = nextValueId
	var haveUnrewrittenUse = false
	var buf = OperandList.create()
	for j in 0 upto index.useCount(valueId) 'eachUse'
		let record = index.useRecordAt(valueId, k: j)
		if index.useSeqAt(record) < peakSeq and keepsOriginal 'beforePeak'
			haveUnrewrittenUse = true
			continue
		end 'beforePeak'

		let useOpIdx = index.useOpAt(record)
		var block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: index.useBlockAt(record))
		let usePos = positionInBlock(block, opIdx: useOpIdx)
		try block.opRefs.insert(usePos, value: appendOp(module, op: constMaterializationOp(freshId, value: constValue))) otherwise panic("rematerializeConstant: opRefs.insert OOB")
		rewriteOpAt(module, opIdx: useOpIdx, from: valueId, to: freshId, buf: buf)
		// The re-emitted copy derives from the original constant — record it so a
		// rematerialized id in an E5001 blocking set chases back to the constant's source
		// span (the `let`/literal the author wrote), not a Rule-3 panic (see SplitLineage).
		lineage.record(freshId, sourceId: valueId)
		freshId = freshId + 1
	end 'eachUse'

	if not haveUnrewrittenUse 'dropDef'
		dropOriginalDef(module, topo: topo, defOpIdx: defOpIdx, blockLocal: index.defBlockOf(valueId))
	end 'dropDef'

	// A remat that re-emitted NOTHING changed nothing: the potential Φ did not decrease, so the next
	// iteration finds the same peak and re-picks the same value — the driver spins until the runaway
	// bound panics thousands of splits later, naming a symptom instead of the cause. Both scopes make
	// this unreachable — `afterPeakUses` because `isRematVictim`'s `killsValueAtPeak` gate guarantees
	// an after-peak use (a value live at the peak that no before-peak use keeps alive there MUST have
	// one), `everyUse` because a live value has at least one use — so assert it here and a regression
	// in either gate fails LOUDLY at the split that caused it.
	if freshId == nextValueId 'relievedNothing'
		panic("rematerializeConstant: value {valueId} was chosen at the peak (sequence {peakSeq}) but this split rewrote NO use of it, so nothing was re-emitted and the peak's pressure is unchanged — the next iteration would re-pick it forever. An `afterPeakUses` remat must not be chosen for a value whose only uses precede the peak (it is live there only around a back edge — that is what `isRematVictim`'s killsValueAtPeak gate refuses, and what the `everyUse` scope is for), and an `everyUse` remat must not be chosen for a value with no uses at all (it would not be live here).")
	end 'relievedNothing'

	stats.rematerialized = stats.rematerialized + 1
	return freshId
end 'rematerializeConstant'

// The immediate a `movRegImm`/`movRegImm32` materializes.
function constValueOfDef(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex) returns MachineWord
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("constValueOfDef: opIdx not in module.ops")
	match op 'k'
		movRegImm(_, value) then return value
		movRegImm32(_, value) then return value
		prologue to jcc then panic("constValueOfDef: def is not a constant materialization")
		movRegReg to loadRegSlot then panic("constValueOfDef: def is not a constant materialization")
	end 'k'
end 'constValueOfDef'

// Re-emit a constant with the SAME size choice `appendIntImmToReg` makes, so a rematerialized
// constant is byte-identical to the original (movRegImm32 for u32-fitting values, else movRegImm).
function constMaterializationOp(destId ValueId, value MachineWord) returns TargetOp
	if value <= u32.max 'fitsU32'
		return TargetOp.movRegImm32(X64VReg.virtual(destId), value: value)
	end 'fitsU32'
	return TargetOp.movRegImm(X64VReg.virtual(destId), value: value)
end 'constMaterializationOp'

// Remove the original constant def's op ref from its block (it is now dead — every use was
// rewritten to a rematerialized copy). The op stays in `module.ops` but unreferenced (harmless,
// exactly like the reuse-copy/edge-move ops SsaDestruction appends).
function dropOriginalDef(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, defOpIdx OpIndex, blockLocal CfgIndex)
	var block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: blockLocal)
	_ = try block.opRefs.remove(positionInBlock(block, opIdx: defOpIdx)) otherwise panic("dropOriginalDef: opRefs.remove OOB")
end 'dropOriginalDef'

// ==========================================================================
// Shared op / block plumbing
// ==========================================================================

// Rewrite one op's uses of `from` to `to` (a reloaded / rematerialized copy). The one op that
// reads `from` but must NOT be rewritten is a spill STORE: it deliberately reads the ORIGINAL
// value to write it to memory, and is emitted BEFORE the reload — rewriting it to `to` would
// make it read a value defined later (a use-before-def). Since a `storeSlotReg`'s source is
// ALWAYS an original spilled value (never a reload), skipping every spill store is exactly right
// and touches no genuine use. A no-op when the op does not use `from` at all.
function rewriteOpAt(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex, from ValueId, to ValueId, buf OperandList)
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("rewriteOpAt: opIdx not in module.ops")
	match spillStoreSlotOf(op) 'spillStore'
		slot then return
		none then break 'spillStore'
	end 'spillStore'
	if not opUsesValue(op, valueId: from, buf: buf) 'noUse'
		return
	end 'noUse'
	try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: substituteValueInOp(op, from: from, to: to)) otherwise panic("rewriteOpAt: ops.set OOB")
end 'rewriteOpAt'

function opUsesValue(op TargetOp, valueId ValueId, buf OperandList) returns bool
	targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if (not operandIsDef(o)) and operandIsVirtual(o) and operandValueId(o) == valueId 'use'
			return true
		end 'use'
	end 'eachOperand'
	return false
end 'opUsesValue'

// Replace virtual `from` with virtual `to` in a vreg operand; a physical reg or any other
// virtual passes through.
function substituteVReg(vreg X64VReg, from ValueId, to ValueId) returns X64VReg
	return match vreg 'v'
		physical gives vreg
		virtual(id) gives X64VReg.virtual(to) if id == from else vreg
	end 'v'
end 'substituteVReg'

// Rebuild an op with every `virtual(from)` operand replaced by `virtual(to)`. EXHAUSTIVE over
// every TargetOp — the same one-place-per-op discipline as `colorTargetOp`, so a new op is a
// compile error here rather than a silently un-rewritten operand. Ops with no register operand
// (frame / control / call) pass through.
function substituteValueInOp(op TargetOp, from ValueId, to ValueId) returns TargetOp
	match op 'rewrite'
		prologue to jcc then return op
		movRegImm(dest, value) then return TargetOp.movRegImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), value: value)
		movRegImm32(dest, value) then return TargetOp.movRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), value: value)
		movRegReg(destReg, srcReg) then return TargetOp.movRegReg(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		movReg32Reg32(destReg, srcReg) then return TargetOp.movReg32Reg32(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		subRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.subRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		imulRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.imulRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		negReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.negReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		cmpRegReg(lhsReg, rhsReg) then return TargetOp.cmpRegReg(substituteVReg(lhsReg, from: from, to: to), rhsReg: substituteVReg(rhsReg, from: from, to: to))
		leaRegRegReg(dest, base, index) then return TargetOp.leaRegRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), index: substituteVReg(index, from: from, to: to))
		leaRegRegImm32(dest, base, disp) then return TargetOp.leaRegRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), disp: disp)
		imulRegRegImm32(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.imulRegRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		cmpRegImm32(reg, imm) then return TargetOp.cmpRegImm32(substituteVReg(reg, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		xchgRegReg to iatCall then return op
		cqo then return op
		idivReg(divisor) then return TargetOp.idivReg(substituteVReg(divisor, from: from, to: to))
		pushReg to popReg then return op
		storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg) then return TargetOp.storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		loadRegSlot(destReg, slot) then return TargetOp.loadRegSlot(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), slot: slot)
	end 'rewrite'
end 'substituteValueInOp'

// The position of `opIdx` within `block.opRefs` (body ops only — spill/reload/remat are always
// body ops, never terminators). Re-derived at every insertion because the positions SHIFT as ops
// are spliced in, which is exactly why the index does not cache them.
function positionInBlock(block IrBlock, opIdx OpIndex) returns ByteCount
	for pos in 0 upto block.opRefs.count() 'each'
		if (try block.opRefs.get(pos) otherwise panic("positionInBlock: opRefs.get OOB")) == opIdx 'match'
			return pos
		end 'match'
	end 'each'
	panic("positionInBlock: op {opIdx} not in block '{block.label}' opRefs (a terminator or wrong block?)")
end 'positionInBlock'

// ==========================================================================
// Rule 2 verification (the hot-overflow E5001 diagnostic lives in
// RegisterPressureDiagnostic.maxon, thrown from the `chooseVictim` -> `none` arm above)
// ==========================================================================

// Verify an inserted spill op is where its placement mode allows.
//
// A COLD spill must land outside every loop: it relieves a FULL-pool overflow by moving a value
// that is IDLE across the pressured region, so its store runs once before the loop and its reload
// once after — zero instructions in the loop body. A cold op landing at depth ≥ 1 would be a
// silent hot spill, so it panics.
//
// A FORCED spill has NO depth restriction, and that is the amended Rule 2. It relieves a
// fixed-register point (a call, an `idiv`) whose live-across set exceeds the registers the op
// leaves alone. The value CANNOT stay in a register there — memory is the only home — so the
// store/reload bracket is a forced placement, not a chosen one, and the allocator searches
// nothing. It is admitted inside a loop because the pair is cheap against the op it brackets: one
// store and one load against a call that costs far more. Refusing it would be a FALSE E5001 —
// the program fits the machine, and the "restructuring" it would demand (hoist the values into an
// array) emits the same store and load at EVERY use, i.e. strictly worse code.
function assertPlacementLegal(cfg FuncCfg, blockLocal CfgIndex, valueId ValueId, what String, placement SpillPlacement)
	match placement 'p'
		forced then return
		cold then break 'p'
	end 'p'

	let depth = try cfg.loopDepth.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("assertPlacementLegal: loopDepth.get OOB")
	if depth != 0 'inLoop'
		panic("assertPlacementLegal: a COLD {what} for value {valueId} would land in block local {blockLocal} at loop depth {depth} — a cold spill relieves a FULL-pool overflow and must lie outside every loop. (A fixed-register point's FORCED spill is the placement that IS allowed in a loop; it goes through SpillPlacement.forced.)")
	end 'inLoop'
end 'assertPlacementLegal'
